 ],
 "literal": "I ache, I indeed! May the morning waste away! / Smeared a small space like the footprint of a female elephant, / his beloved wife placed upon the grass / a sweet small ball of rice; how did he eat it? / With gates opened for the world to enter, / he who was accustomed to eating with many people!",
 "translation": "I suffer, indeed! May this morning perish! How could he eat that sweet, tiny ball of rice, placed upon the grass by his beloved wife, after she smeared a small space no larger than a female elephant's footprint? He, who was accustomed to eating with many people, keeping his gates wide open for the whole world to enter!",
 "modern": "நான் மிகவும் வருந்துகிறேன்! இந்த விடியற்காலை பொழுது அழியட்டும்! பெண் யானையின் காலடி போன்ற சிறிய இடத்தை மெழுகி, அவனது அன்பான மனைவி தர்பைப் புல்லின் மேல் வைத்த அந்த இனிய சிறிய சோற்றுப் பிண்டத்தை அவன் எவ்வாறு உண்டானோ? உலகமே உள்ளே நுழையும்படி தன் வாயிலைத் திறந்து வைத்து, எப்போதும் பலரோடு சேர்ந்து உண்பதை வழக்கமாகக் கொண்டிருந்தானே அவன்!",
 "explanation": "This poem is an elegy (kaiyaṟunilai) composed by the poet Veḷḷerukkilaiyār on the death of his patron, the generous chieftain Veḷ Evvi. The poet expresses his intense grief, wishing that the morning would never dawn now that his patron is gone. He describes the funerary ritual where Evvi's grieving wife has cleared a tiny patch of ground, the size of a female elephant's footprint, and placed a small ball of rice (piṇṭam) on the grass as an offering to the deceased. The poet laments how Evvi, who was famous for keeping his palace gates wide open to feed the entire world and who never ate without the company of many guests, could now accept such a tiny, solitary offering. The contrast between Evvi's grand, open-handed life and his meager, silent death forms the emotional core of the poem.",
